Start the stream on time, with no silence of the core before it

The stream waits for the date of its first block before it plays it,
filling buffers with silence until then. Three things went wrong:

- The delay reported to the core left out that wait. The core then took
  the stream for early and inserted silence of its own, dated at the start
  date, which the stream played after its own wait: the audio came late,
  the core flushed it, and the same happened again, for ever. A file whose
  audio starts after its video (an empty edit in MP4, such as the
  LG Optimus 3D's MOV004.mp4, 0.666 s) played no sound until a seek.
  No delay is reported until the stream has started.
- The silence before the first samples was not counted in the size of the
  buffer: the buffers of the wait were empty, and the first one lost as
  many samples at its end as it had silence at its beginning.
- The start was computed from timing data taken at the beginning of a
  cycle for a buffer the graph asked for in the middle of one. Until the
  start, a buffer holds only what the graph asks for in its cycle.

The original is Debian's vlc-plugin-pipewire 3-4; VLC's own module
(master) reports no timing while starting either.
---
diff -urN a/modules/audio_output/pipewire.c b/modules/audio_output/pipewire.c
index fc366b5..affd28e 100644
--- a/modules/audio_output/pipewire.c
+++ b/modules/audio_output/pipewire.c
@@ -158,9 +158,18 @@ static void stream_process(void *data)
         struct spa_chunk *chunk = d->chunk;
         unsigned char *dst = d->data;
         size_t frame_room = d->maxsize / s->stride;
-        size_t room = frame_room * s->stride;
+        size_t room;
         block_t *block;
 
+        /*
+         * Until the start, fill only what the graph asks for in this cycle, so
+         * that the start falls at the beginning of a cycle, which is when the
+         * timing data was taken.
+         */
+        if (s->starting && b->requested != 0 && b->requested < frame_room)
+            frame_room = b->requested;
+        room = frame_room * s->stride;
+
         chunk->offset = 0;
         chunk->stride = s->stride;
         chunk->size = 0;
@@ -197,6 +206,9 @@ static void stream_process(void *data)
             memset(dst, 0, skip);
             dst += skip;
             room -= skip;
+            chunk->size += skip;
+            /* The silence plays before the queued samples, it adds to the delay */
+            s->time.frames += skip / s->stride;
         }
 
         while ((block = s->queue.head) != NULL) {
@@ -299,8 +311,15 @@ static int vlc_pw_stream_get_time(struct vlc_pw_stream *s,
     int ret = -1;
 
     vlc_pw_lock(s->context);
+    /*
+     * Until the first sample is played, the delay is that of the start date,
+     * which the stream waits for (see stream_process()), not that of the
+     * stream. Report none, so that the core does not insert silence of its own
+     * before the stream starts.
+     */
     if (pw_stream_get_state(s->stream, NULL) == PW_STREAM_STATE_STREAMING
-     && s->time.pts != VLC_TS_INVALID) {
+     && s->time.pts != VLC_TS_INVALID
+     && s->start != VLC_TS_INVALID && !s->starting) {
         mtime_t elapsed = mdate() - s->time.pts;
         *delay = ((s->time.frames * CLOCK_FREQ) / s->time.rate) - elapsed;
         ret = 0;
